---
// The sleep block, read back off the watch that was awake for it.
//
// Why this is on week 1 at all. The day sheet above works for every hour the
// student was conscious: it is filled in as the day goes, which is the whole
// instruction. Sleep is the one block of the twenty-four hours that cannot be
// filled in as it happens, so it is the only part of the day that gets
// reconstructed in the morning from memory. A watch or a phone was awake for
// it and wrote down what it thought was going on. This reads that back.
//
// It keeps week 1 a baseline. Nothing here asks the student to sleep
// differently, to sleep at a different time, or to go to bed knowing they are
// being measured. The night on the screen has already happened and is finished.
//
// What it must not do, and this is the constraint that shaped everything else.
//
// Week 3 is the two instruments week: one night recorded by a device and by a
// paper diary, and the student reports the disagreement in minutes without
// resolving it. Rule 4 in CLAUDE.md gives each week its own method and no two
// weeks may share one, so this component compares nothing. It does not score
// the night, does not put a device's number beside anything the student typed,
// does not say better or worse and does not say how far apart two sources are.
// It reports what one instrument recorded and stops. That restraint is also
// why a night held by two sources is shown one source at a time: laying a phone
// over a watch is not a neater timeline, it is week 3 three weeks early.
//
// It also interprets nothing. Wrist staging is a guess made from movement and a
// pulse, not a recording of a brain, and telling a student their deep sleep was
// short would be personalised clinical advice, which CLAUDE.md puts out of
// scope. Every number on the page is attributed to the instrument that produced
// it, in those words.
//
// Scope. An export holds years, and the course is about one day. So the date is
// asked for before the file is, and the scan keeps only the records overlapping
// that one night: not the week around it, not a second night for context, not a
// trend. Everything else in the file goes past without being retained, which is
// the scope rule and the privacy promise in the same mechanism.
//
// Midday to midday, because "the night of the fifteenth" has to mean one thing
// and a night crosses midnight. That window also takes in the afternoon of the
// day chosen, so an afternoon sleep turns up in it. Rather than hide that, the
// reader splits the window into the stretches the instrument actually recorded
// and names the long one as the night, so nobody is told their sleep began at
// ten past three in the afternoon.
//
// Size is the real engineering problem. export.xml is routinely hundreds of
// megabytes, because it holds every heart rate sample the person has ever
// taken, and DOMParser or a single read into a string would kill the tab. The
// scanning lives in src/lib/health-export.ts: slices from the blob, a streaming
// TextDecoder, a carry buffer across the boundary so a record cut in half is
// not lost, and everything that is not a sleep record dropped as it goes. The
// boundary case is the bug this would actually have, so spec/health-export
// reads the same file at every chunk size down to one byte and requires the
// same night out of all of them.
//
// The zip is refused deliberately. Health exports export.zip holding export.xml
// and export_cda.xml, and unzipping in the browser would be a dependency and a
// second decompression of a file this size for the sake of one instruction. So
// the page asks for the unzipped file and, if it is handed a zip or handed the
// clinical document instead, says which file it wants rather than finding
// nothing and looking broken.
//
// Privacy is the same promise the sheet above makes, and it is kept the same
// way: read in the page, nothing sent, nothing written to the browser, all of
// it gone on reload. This site is static files on Pages, so there is no server
// here to receive an upload even in principle. The single network request this
// component can make is for the sample night, and only when a reader presses
// the button that says so.
//
// The sample night is the opt out, and it is not a lesser route. A student
// without a watch, or one who would rather not hand a health export to a web
// page, reads a published night instead and takes the same thing out of it. The
// file is synthetic and says so in its own first comment.
//
// What it cannot do: fill the sheet in. DayRecorder is another agent's file
// this week, so the two times come out as text for the student to carry up the
// page by hand.
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";

const SAMPLE = "/samples/apple-health-sleep-sample.xml";
---

<section
  class="night"
  data-sleep-export
  data-sample={withBase(SAMPLE)}
  aria-labelledby="night-title"
>
  <h3 id="night-title">The night the watch was awake for</h3>

  <p class="night-lead">
    One night, read off an Apple Health export. It compares nothing and keeps nothing.
  </p>

  <div class="night-how">
    <h4>How to read one back</h4>

    <p class="night-help">
      On the phone: Health, then your own picture at the top, then Export All Health Data. That
      gives you <b>export.zip</b>. Unzip it and choose <b>export.xml</b> from inside it, which is
      the large one. This page does not open zip files and does not want the other file in there.
    </p>

    <p class="night-help">
      The date goes in first, because the file holds years and this page will only ever open one
      night of it. The night it means is the one that closes that day: everything the export
      recorded between midday on the date you give it and midday the next.
    </p>

    <p class="night-help">
      There is a route through this week that needs no watch and no export, and it costs you
      nothing: fill the sleep block on the sheet above from memory, the way you fill the rest of the
      day. That is the week's actual method, and memory is the instrument week 1 is built on. If you
      would like to see what a device records without handing over your own file, press the button
      for the sample night below, or take{" "}
      <a href={withBase(SAMPLE)} download>the sample export</a> and choose it as the file. It is a
      night that was generated rather than slept: it describes nobody.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="night-work">
    <div class="night-head">
      <h4>The night</h4>

      <label class="night-date">
        <span class="field-name">Night of</span>
        <input type="date" data-night-date />
      </label>
    </div>

    <label class="night-file">
      <span class="field-name">Your export.xml file</span>
      <input type="file" accept=".xml,text/xml,application/xml" data-night-file />
    </label>

    <p class="night-actions">
      <button type="button" class="night-read" data-action="read" disabled>Read that night</button>
      <button type="button" data-action="sample">Read the sample night instead</button>
    </p>

    <p class="night-progress" data-progress hidden></p>
    <p class="night-status" role="status" data-status></p>

    <div class="night-out" data-out hidden></div>

    <p class="night-privacy">
      Nothing here is saved and nothing is sent. Your export is read inside this tab, a slice at a
      time, and this site is a set of static files with no server behind it to receive one. Nothing
      is written to this browser, and a reload loses every bit of it. The only request this page can
      make is for the sample night, and only when you press the button for it. Same promise as the
      sheet, and the same policy behind it:{" "}
      <a href={withBase("/policies/#the-records-you-make-are-yours")}>the records you make</a> are
      yours.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* The outline is deliberate. Do not remove it.
   *
   * Each component on this page is a box, and the box is what says where one
   * tool ends and the next begins: the day sheet above and this block below it
   * are two instruments for two halves of the same twenty-four hours, and
   * without an edge apiece they read as one long undifferentiated stack. The
   * shared visual language lives inside the boxes rather than instead of them.
   *
   * This is written down because it has now been misread twice. The standing
   * instruction on this site against a rounded corner block was aimed at an
   * inner block inside the day sheet, not at the outer container, and on the
   * other reading both components came within a commit of losing their edges.
   * No cards inside the box; the box itself stays.
   *
   * The four values are .recorder's own, read from DayRecorder.astro so that
   * the two are visibly one kind of object rather than two near misses. If that
   * component's box changes, this one changes with it. */
  .night {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    padding: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
  }

  .night h3 {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xs);
  }

  .night h4 {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
  }

  .night-lead {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-xl);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  /* Two zones, and the boundary between them is the loudest thing in the
     component. Everything that tells a reader what to do is above it and
     nothing below it is prose bar the promise at the foot, which is the
     arrangement the day sheet arrived at. Sixty four pixels of air, then the
     rule, then thirty two: twice the largest gap inside either zone, so the
     division cannot be read as just another paragraph break. */
  .night-work {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-2xl);
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  /* The masthead: what this zone is, and the one piece of metadata that
     identifies the night, in the corner on the same line. Baseline alignment
     rather than centres, so the label sits on the heading's own line and drops
     under it when there is no room for both. The rule beneath belongs to the
     pair rather than to the heading, which is why it is on the wrapper. */
  .night-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-xs) var(--at-spacing-md);
    margin-block-end: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    padding-block-end: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  .night .night-head h4 {
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* Instructions, not prose: one step smaller and quieter than the lesson
     around them. In the upper zone they are the whole content, so they take the
     measure rather than sitting beside a control. */
  .night-help {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .night-help:last-child {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }

  .field-name {
    display: block;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .night-date {
    display: block;
    min-width: 9rem;
  }

  .night input[type="date"] {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.3rem 0.45rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .night input[type="date"]:hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 50%, var(--at-border));
  }

  .night input[type="date"]:focus-visible {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 8%, var(--at-bg));
  }

  .night :is(input[type="date"])::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator {
    margin-inline-start: 0.3rem;
    padding: 0;
    opacity: 0.45;
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .night :is(input[type="date"]):is(:hover, :focus-within)::-webkit-calendar-picker-indicator {
    opacity: 1;
  }

  /* The file field.
   *
   * It stays a real <input type="file">, because everything it does for free is
   * worth more than the look of it: the system picker, the keyboard, the drop
   * target over the whole control, and the name of the chosen file announced
   * without a line of script. What it does not have to do is arrive in the
   * browser's own 1997 chrome next to type that has been set with some care, so
   * the control takes the same bordered box every other field here wears and
   * ::file-selector-button gives its button the same edge as the buttons under
   * it. Nothing below changes what the control does.
   *
   * Firefox draws the button but not the same way, and Safari differs again.
   * All three are painted here and none of them is relied on: with every one of
   * these declarations dropped the field still opens a picker and still takes a
   * drop. */
  .night-file {
    display: block;
    margin-block-end: var(--at-spacing-md);
  }

  .night input[type="file"] {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.35rem 0.45rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .night input[type="file"]:hover {
    border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 50%, var(--at-border));
  }

  .night input[type="file"]::file-selector-button {
    margin-inline-end: 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .night input[type="file"]:hover::file-selector-button {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  /* Stated rather than inherited: every control in here is an unstyled element
     the theme never sees. */
  .night :is(input, button, a, summary):focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  /* The buttons take the line they are on, the way the sheet's do: an auto-fit
     grid, so two fill the row on a laptop and stack to full width on a phone
     rather than huddling at the left in their own text widths. */
  .night-actions {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(13rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
  }

  .night button {
    padding: 0.45rem 0.7rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    border-radius: var(--at-border-radius);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    color: var(--at-text);
    font: inherit;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .night button:hover:not(:disabled) {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .night button:disabled {
    cursor: not-allowed;
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  /* The repeated action carries the brand and the weight, the plain one beside
     it does not. Disabled it drops back to the quiet treatment rather than
     wearing a dimmed brand, since a washed out gold reads as a broken button
     instead of an unavailable one. The words are the first channel either way:
     the label says Read that night in both states. */
  .night .night-read:not(:disabled) {
    border-color: var(--at-primary);
    background: var(--at-primary);
    color: var(--at-on-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .night .night-read:not(:disabled):hover {
    border-color: var(--at-primary-hover);
    background: var(--at-primary-hover);
  }

  /* Progress while a file is being read, and what happened after. Quiet, and
     the same size as the field labels they sit under, because neither is
     something to read twice. */
  .night-progress,
  .night-status {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .night-status:empty {
    display: none;
  }

  .night-status[data-tone="wrong"] {
    color: var(--at-warning);
  }

  /* The night itself, once there is one. A rule above it, because what follows
     is a different kind of thing from the controls that produced it, and the
     component has no other way left to say so. */
  .night-out:not([hidden]) {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-xl);
    padding-block-start: var(--at-spacing-lg);
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
  }

  .night-privacy {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl) 0;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  /* The source picker. A night written by two instruments is two records, and
     this is where the reader chooses which one to look at, one at a time. No
     box around it: the legend is a field label like every other, and the
     options sit in a row under it. */
  .night-out :global(fieldset) {
    margin: 0 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
  }

  .night-out :global(legend) {
    padding: 0;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    line-height: 1.6;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .night-out :global(.night-sources) {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-xs) var(--at-spacing-lg);
  }

  .night-out :global(.night-sources label) {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  .night-out :global(input[type="radio"]) {
    accent-color: var(--at-primary);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .night-out :global(p) {
    margin-block: 0 var(--at-spacing-md);
  }

  /* The two figures.
   *
   * Both are drawings and both are aria-hidden, so each is followed by a table
   * that is in the accessibility tree and off the screen. Neither prints, and
   * that is not an oversight: the day sheet's own print rule hides every child
   * of #main that is not the sheet, so a print of this page is the sheet and
   * nothing else. It is the right behaviour to inherit. The file a student
   * keeps is the sheet with two times written into it, and a second document
   * would muddy what "the only copy" means in the promise at the foot of this
   * block.
   *
   * Colour is one hue in both. A four step ramp on this brand's gold measures
   * deltaE 11.0 between its middle pair against a floor of 15, so it is not a
   * palette a reader could use; and the states are not ordered anyway, so a
   * ramp would be asserting a scale the instrument cannot support. Position in
   * the lanes and arc length in the ring carry the meaning instead.
   */
  .night-out :global(.night-fig) {
    margin: var(--at-spacing-lg) 0;
  }

  /* The lanes.
   *
   * Row geometry lives in these three custom properties because the stitches
   * are positioned against it in script: a stitch has to start exactly at the
   * bottom edge of one capsule and stop at the top edge of the next, and doing
   * that arithmetic in two places would guarantee they drift apart. */
  .night-out :global(.lanes) {
    --lane-row: 1.4rem;
    --lane-gap: 0.35rem;
    --lane-inset: 0.28rem;

    display: flex;
    gap: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-names) {
    display: flex;
    flex: none;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--lane-gap);
    width: 4.25rem;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-name) {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: flex-end;
    height: var(--lane-row);
    overflow: hidden;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-plot) {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-rows) {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--lane-gap);
  }

  /* The hour rules and the stitches, behind the capsules. First in the DOM so
     that a capsule always wins the overlap without anyone having to invent a
     stacking context for it. */
  .night-out :global(.lane-under) {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-row) {
    position: relative;
    height: var(--lane-row);
  }

  /* Capsules rather than rectangles, which is most of what makes a night read
     as a night. Four pixels minimum because a four minute stretch inside a
     seven hour one is two pixels wide on a phone and a stretch that cannot be
     seen is a stretch the figure has dropped. That is a real distortion of
     anything under about five minutes, taken knowingly, and the hidden table
     carries the exact lengths. */
  .night-out :global(.lane-capsule) {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: var(--lane-inset);
    min-width: 4px;
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-rule) {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--at-border);
    transform: translateX(-0.5px);
  }

  /* The stitch. Drawn in the gutter and never across a row it does not claim,
     so it says these two stretches met and nothing more. */
  .night-out :global(.lane-stitch) {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--at-primary) 60%, transparent);
    transform: translateX(-0.5px);
  }

  /* No baseline rule: the hour lines already carry the axis, and a heavy line
     under the lanes would be the loudest thing in a quiet figure. */
  .night-out :global(.lane-axis) {
    position: relative;
    height: 1.1rem;
    margin-block-start: 0.2rem;
  }

  .night-out :global(.lane-tick) {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* The ring. Sized in real pixels by the script, so this only has to stop it
     overflowing a narrow column. */
  .night-out :global(.ring) {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .night-out :global(.ring-arc) {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--at-primary);
  }

  .night-out :global(.ring-leader) {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--at-border);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }

  /* Text wears text tokens. The arcs are all one colour, so a label tinted to
     match its arc would say nothing and would cost contrast. */
  .night-out :global(.ring-label) {
    dominant-baseline: middle;
    fill: var(--at-text);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .night-out :global(.ring-length) {
    fill: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .night-out :global(.ring-centre) {
    dominant-baseline: middle;
    fill: var(--at-text);
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .night-out :global(.ring-centre-word) {
    fill: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-variant-numeric: normal;
  }

  /* Forced colours throws a background away, which would empty both figures.
     The system's own ink is what they are drawn in there. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .night-out :global(.lane-capsule),
    .night-out :global(.lane-stitch) {
      background: CanvasText;
    }

    .night-out :global(.ring-arc) {
      stroke: CanvasText;
    }
  }

  .night-out :global(figcaption) {
    margin-block-start: var(--at-spacing-sm);
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-sm);
  }

  /* In the accessibility tree, off the screen. Never display: none, which would
     take it out of the tree as well and leave a drawing with no text at all. */
  .night-out :global(.night-hidden) {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .night-out :global(h4) {
    margin-block: var(--at-spacing-xl) var(--at-spacing-xs);
  }

  .night-out :global(input:focus-visible) {
    outline: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Wiring. Everything that reads the file is in src/lib/health-export.ts,
  // which is a pure module with no DOM in it, so the streaming and the
  // boundary case are tested directly rather than through a page.
  import {
    episodesOf,
    formatClock,
    formatLength,
    groupBySource,
    longestEpisode,
    looksLikeZip,
    STATE_ORDER,
    nightWindowFor,
    scanSleepWindow,
    sliceNight,
    summarise,
    type Episode,
    type NightSlice,
    type SleepSegment,
    type SleepStateKey,
    type SourceNight,
  } from "../lib/health-export";
  import { formatCourseDate } from "../lib/dates";

  // The file the reader chose, as a handle rather than as data: a File is a
  // pointer at something on disk, and holding one keeps nothing in the page.
  // Re-read on demand, so that changing the date is a deliberate act with a
  // button press rather than a night that quietly swaps itself for another.
  let chosen: File | null = null;
  let reading = false;

  const el = <T extends Element>(root: Element, selector: string): T | null =>
    root.querySelector<T>(selector);

  const say = (root: HTMLElement, message: string, tone: "plain" | "wrong" = "plain"): void => {
    const status = el<HTMLElement>(root, "[data-status]");
    if (!status) return;
    status.dataset.tone = tone;
    status.textContent = message;
  };

  const progress = (root: HTMLElement, message: string | null): void => {
    const line = el<HTMLElement>(root, "[data-progress]");
    if (!line) return;
    line.hidden = message === null;
    line.textContent = message ?? "";
  };

  const clearOut = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const out = el<HTMLElement>(root, "[data-out]");
    if (!out) return;
    out.replaceChildren();
    out.hidden = true;
  };

  const make = (tag: string, className?: string, text?: string): HTMLElement => {
    const node = document.createElement(tag);
    if (className) node.className = className;
    if (text !== undefined) node.textContent = text;
    return node;
  };

  /** Wrapped rather than hidden in place: a table sizes to its own contents and
   * ignores a width of one pixel, so the clipped box has to be a plain div
   * around it or the page grows a horizontal scrollbar it never shows. */
  const offScreen = (node: HTMLElement): HTMLElement => {
    const wrap = make("div", "night-hidden");
    wrap.append(node);
    return wrap;
  };

  const megabytes = (bytes: number): string =>
    bytes >= 1024 * 1024
      ? `${Math.round(bytes / (1024 * 1024))} MB`
      : `${Math.max(1, Math.round(bytes / 1024))} kB`;

  /** A date input's value, or today if the reader has not set one. */
  const dateValue = (root: HTMLElement): string => {
    const field = el<HTMLInputElement>(root, "[data-night-date]");
    return field?.value?.trim() ?? "";
  };

  const setDate = (root: HTMLElement, value: string): void => {
    const field = el<HTMLInputElement>(root, "[data-night-date]");
    if (field) field.value = value;
  };

  const readyToRead = (root: HTMLElement): void => {
    const button = el<HTMLButtonElement>(root, "[data-action='read']");
    if (button) button.disabled = !chosen || !nightWindowFor(dateValue(root)) || reading;
  };

  // The row order, and the arc order, are one decision and it lives in
  // src/lib/health-export.ts beside the states themselves, with the reasoning:
  // it is deliberately not a depth scale, and the cost of that is that more of
  // the stitches below have to skip a row than would under Apple's ordering.
  const lanesOf = (segments: SleepSegment[]): SleepStateKey[] => {
    const present = STATE_ORDER.filter((lane) =>
      segments.some((segment) => segment.state === lane),
    );
    const rest = Array.from(
      new Set(
        segments
          .filter((segment) => !STATE_ORDER.includes(segment.state))
          .map((segment) => segment.state),
      ),
    );
    return [...present, ...rest] as SleepStateKey[];
  };

  /**
   * The night as capsules on a shared clock.
   *
   * Drawn after Apple Health's own sleep chart for its form and none of its
   * claims: capsules rather than rectangles so a four minute stretch reads as a
   * lozenge instead of a sliver, no filled lane tracks, hairline hour rules
   * instead of a baseline, and a stitch joining consecutive stretches so the
   * night reads as one run rather than as four independent rows.
   *
   * The stitch is drawn in pieces, one per gutter between rows, and that is the
   * whole of its honesty. A single line from the REM row down to the deep row
   * would cross the core row, and a reader is entitled to read a line crossing
   * a row as passing through it. The night did not pass through core: it went
   * from one stretch straight to the next. Drawn as separate pieces the stitch
   * is absent from every row it does not claim, and present only in the space
   * between them. It is drawn at all only where one stretch ends exactly where
   * the next begins, so it is never asserting a continuity the file does not
   * have.
   *
   * Colour carries nothing here. The row already says which state a capsule is,
   * so a second channel would be redundant, and the four step ramp that would
   * be needed to spend it fails the normal vision floor on this brand's single
   * hue anyway: #e5a74f against #c18427 is deltaE 11.0 against a floor of 15.
   * One hue, and position does the work.
   */
  const laneFigure = (episode: Episode, source: string): HTMLElement => {
    const span = Math.max(1, episode.endWallMs - episode.startWallMs);
    const at = (ms: number): number => ((ms - episode.startWallMs) / span) * 100;
    const rows = lanesOf(episode.segments);

    const figure = make("figure", "night-fig");
    const lanes = make("div", "lanes");
    lanes.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
    lanes.style.setProperty("--lane-count", String(rows.length));

    const names = make("div", "lane-names");
    const plot = make("div", "lane-plot");
    const rowsBox = make("div", "lane-rows");
    const under = make("div", "lane-under");
    rowsBox.append(under);

    for (const lane of rows) {
      const inLane = episode.segments.filter((segment) => segment.state === lane);
      names.append(make("div", "lane-name", inLane[0]?.label ?? lane));
      const row = make("div", "lane-row");
      for (const segment of inLane) {
        const capsule = make("div", "lane-capsule");
        capsule.style.insetInlineStart = `${at(segment.startWallMs)}%`;
        capsule.style.width = `${Math.max(0, at(segment.endWallMs) - at(segment.startWallMs))}%`;
        row.append(capsule);
      }
      rowsBox.append(row);
    }

    // The axis is filled in after the figure is on the page, because how many
    // hours it can name depends on how wide it actually is.
    const axis = make("div", "lane-axis");

    // The stitches. Only between consecutive stretches that meet, and only in
    // the gutters, one piece per row crossed.
    const ordered = episode.segments
      .filter((segment) => segment.state !== "inBed")
      .sort((one, other) => one.startWallMs - other.startWallMs);
    for (let index = 0; index < ordered.length - 1; index += 1) {
      const from = ordered[index];
      const to = ordered[index + 1];
      // A second of slack, which absorbs rounding rather than joining two
      // stretches with a real gap between them.
      if (Math.abs(to.startWallMs - from.endWallMs) > 1000) continue;
      const fromRow = rows.indexOf(from.state);
      const toRow = rows.indexOf(to.state);
      if (fromRow < 0 || toRow < 0 || fromRow === toRow) continue;
      for (let row = Math.min(fromRow, toRow); row < Math.max(fromRow, toRow); row += 1) {
        const stitch = make("div", "lane-stitch");
        stitch.style.insetInlineStart = `${at(to.startWallMs)}%`;
        stitch.style.insetBlockStart =
          `calc((var(--lane-row) + var(--lane-gap)) * ${row} + var(--lane-row) - var(--lane-inset))`;
        stitch.style.height = "calc(var(--lane-gap) + var(--lane-inset) * 2)";
        under.append(stitch);
      }
    }

    plot.append(rowsBox, axis);
    lanes.append(names, plot);
    figure.append(lanes);
    figure.append(
      make(
        "figcaption",
        undefined,
        `Each row is a state this export names and each capsule is one stretch ${source} put in that state, drawn at the clock time it recorded. A hairline joins two stretches that ran back to back, and skips any row it does not touch. Recorded by ${source}; read from the file you chose.`,
      ),
    );
    return figure;
  };

  /**
   * The hour marks, decided from the width the figure really got.
   *
   * This runs after the figure is in the page rather than while it is being
   * built, because the answer is a measurement: an hourly mark is twenty eight
   * pixels apart in a phone's column and all eight labels sit on top of each
   * other. Measured at 390px that is exactly what happened, which is the whole
   * reason this is a second pass. The step is whatever keeps a label clear of
   * its neighbour, and a mark whose label would hang off either end of the plot
   * is dropped rather than shifted, since a time label that is not centred on
   * its own rule is pointing at the wrong minute.
   */
  const addHourAxis = (figure: HTMLElement, episode: Episode): void => {
    const plot = figure.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".lane-plot");
    const under = figure.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".lane-under");
    const axis = figure.querySelector<HTMLElement>(".lane-axis");
    if (!plot || !under || !axis) return;
    const width = plot.clientWidth;
    const span = Math.max(1, episode.endWallMs - episode.startWallMs);
    if (width < 40) return;

    const probe = make("div", "lane-tick", "00:00");
    axis.append(probe);
    const labelWidth = probe.getBoundingClientRect().width || 36;
    probe.remove();

    const hour = 3_600_000;
    const perHour = (width / span) * hour;
    const step = Math.max(1, Math.ceil((labelWidth + 14) / Math.max(perHour, 1)));
    const first = Math.ceil(episode.startWallMs / hour) * hour;

    for (let tick = first; tick <= episode.endWallMs; tick += step * hour) {
      const fraction = (tick - episode.startWallMs) / span;
      const rule = make("div", "lane-rule");
      rule.style.insetInlineStart = `${fraction * 100}%`;
      under.append(rule);
      const centre = fraction * width;
      if (centre - labelWidth / 2 < 0 || centre + labelWidth / 2 > width) continue;
      const mark = make("div", "lane-tick", formatClock(tick));
      mark.style.insetInlineStart = `${fraction * 100}%`;
      axis.append(mark);
    }
  };

  /**
   * The same night as parts of a whole.
   *
   * A ring rather than the stacked bar the form guidance defaults to, because
   * the author asked for one and because this is the case where a ring is
   * allowed: at most six parts, read at a glance, and parts that differ by an
   * order of magnitude rather than the close values a ring hides. That last
   * point is what separates it from the pie this repo already refused in
   * CertaintyOfStrong, where four of five categories sat at 18, 18, 24 and 31
   * and no reader could judge the angles.
   *
   * What the arcs are is decided in src/lib/health-export.ts and is the only
   * interesting part: in bed is never an arc, because it is the envelope the
   * other states sit inside and adding it to asleep would draw a fifteen hour
   * night. The whole is the span of the record and every minute of it is
   * accounted for, including the minutes the instrument left unlabelled.
   *
   * Every arc is the same hue, separated by the two pixel surface gap the
   * visualisation guidance asks for and labelled directly with its own name and
   * length. That is not a fallback from a palette that failed: a ramp would
   * assert an order these states do not have, and the ramp fails on measurement
   * as well, so colour here carries nothing and arc length plus the label carry
   * all of it.
   */
  const ringFigure = (slices: NightSlice[], source: string, width: number): HTMLElement => {
    const figure = make("figure", "night-fig");
    const total = slices.reduce((sum, slice) => sum + slice.minutes, 0);
    if (total <= 0 || slices.length === 0) return figure;

    // Laid out in real pixels rather than in a scaled viewBox, so the labels are
    // the size they are asked to be on a phone as well as on a laptop. The
    // viewBox is the rendered box, so one user unit is one CSS pixel.
    const W = Math.max(250, Math.min(width, 520));
    const LABEL = 74;
    const LEADER = 12;
    const outer = Math.max(44, Math.min((W - 2 * (LABEL + LEADER) - 6) / 2, 104));
    const band = Math.max(13, outer * 0.3);
    const mid = outer - band / 2;
    const H = Math.round(2 * outer + 60);
    const cx = W / 2;
    const cy = H / 2;
    const circumference = 2 * Math.PI * mid;
    const GAP = 2;

    const svgNs = "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg";
    const svg = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "svg");
    svg.setAttribute("class", "ring");
    svg.setAttribute("viewBox", `0 0 ${Math.round(W)} ${H}`);
    svg.setAttribute("width", String(Math.round(W)));
    svg.setAttribute("height", String(H));
    svg.setAttribute("aria-hidden", "true");
    svg.setAttribute("focusable", "false");

    const arcs = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "g");
    arcs.setAttribute("transform", `rotate(-90 ${cx} ${cy})`);
    svg.append(arcs);

    const sides: { left: Label[]; right: Label[] } = { left: [], right: [] };
    interface Label {
      slice: NightSlice;
      y: number;
      anchorX: number;
      edgeX: number;
      edgeY: number;
      side: 1 | -1;
    }

    let run = 0;
    for (const slice of slices) {
      const length = (slice.minutes / total) * circumference;
      // Two pixels of surface between arcs, and never less than a sliver of
      // arc left over: a three minute stretch is a hairline on this ring and a
      // hairline is still the honest width for three minutes.
      const drawn = Math.max(length - GAP, 1.5);
      const arc = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "circle");
      arc.setAttribute("class", "ring-arc");
      arc.setAttribute("cx", String(cx));
      arc.setAttribute("cy", String(cy));
      arc.setAttribute("r", String(mid));
      arc.setAttribute("stroke-width", String(band));
      arc.setAttribute("stroke-dasharray", `${drawn} ${circumference - drawn}`);
      arc.setAttribute("stroke-dashoffset", String(-(run + GAP / 2)));
      arcs.append(arc);

      const angle = ((run + length / 2) / circumference) * 2 * Math.PI - Math.PI / 2;
      const side: 1 | -1 = Math.cos(angle) >= 0 ? 1 : -1;
      sides[side === 1 ? "right" : "left"].push({
        slice,
        y: cy + outer * Math.sin(angle),
        anchorX: side === 1 ? cx + outer + LEADER + 4 : cx - outer - LEADER - 4,
        edgeX: cx + outer * Math.cos(angle),
        edgeY: cy + outer * Math.sin(angle),
        side,
      });
      run += length;
    }

    // Labels pushed apart where two arcs meet at nearly the same angle, so a
    // short stretch beside a long one still has a readable name.
    // Two lines of label at thirteen pixels come to twenty eight, so anything
    // under about thirty four has them touching rather than merely close.
    const SPACING = 34;
    for (const key of ["left", "right"] as const) {
      const list = sides[key].sort((one, other) => one.y - other.y);
      for (let index = 1; index < list.length; index += 1) {
        if (list[index].y - list[index - 1].y < SPACING) {
          list[index].y = list[index - 1].y + SPACING;
        }
      }
      const overflow = list.length ? list[list.length - 1].y - (H - 16) : 0;
      if (overflow > 0) for (const label of list) label.y -= overflow;
      for (const label of list) {
        const leader = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "polyline");
        leader.setAttribute("class", "ring-leader");
        leader.setAttribute(
          "points",
          `${label.edgeX},${label.edgeY} ${label.edgeX + label.side * LEADER * 0.6},${label.y} ${label.anchorX - label.side * 4},${label.y}`,
        );
        svg.append(leader);

        const text = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "text");
        text.setAttribute("class", "ring-label");
        text.setAttribute("x", String(label.anchorX));
        text.setAttribute("y", String(label.y));
        text.setAttribute("text-anchor", label.side === 1 ? "start" : "end");
        const name = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "tspan");
        name.textContent = label.slice.label;
        name.setAttribute("x", String(label.anchorX));
        const length = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "tspan");
        length.setAttribute("class", "ring-length");
        length.setAttribute("x", String(label.anchorX));
        length.setAttribute("dy", "1.15em");
        length.textContent = formatLength(label.slice.minutes);
        text.append(name, length);
        svg.append(text);
      }
    }

    const centre = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "text");
    centre.setAttribute("class", "ring-centre");
    centre.setAttribute("x", String(cx));
    centre.setAttribute("y", String(cy - 8));
    centre.setAttribute("text-anchor", "middle");
    const centreValue = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "tspan");
    centreValue.setAttribute("x", String(cx));
    centreValue.textContent = formatLength(total);
    const centreWord = document.createElementNS(svgNs, "tspan");
    centreWord.setAttribute("class", "ring-centre-word");
    centreWord.setAttribute("x", String(cx));
    centreWord.setAttribute("dy", "1.3em");
    centreWord.textContent = "in the record";
    centre.append(centreValue, centreWord);
    svg.append(centre);

    figure.append(svg);
    figure.append(
      make(
        "figcaption",
        undefined,
        `The same night as parts of one whole: each arc is one part at the length ${source} recorded for it, labelled with its own name. The parts do not overlap, and together they are the whole of the record. Recorded by ${source}; read from the file you chose.`,
      ),
    );
    return figure;
  };

  /**
   * Every stretch, in words, kept in the accessibility tree and off the screen.
   *
   * Sixteen rows of times is not something a sighted reader is going to work
   * through, and the author is right that it was the heaviest thing on the
   * page. It is not, however, decoration: the lane figure is aria-hidden, so
   * this table is the only account of it a screen reader ever gets, and the
   * build's axe pass cannot catch its loss because axe never sees anything
   * built in script. So it is hidden the way a caption for a drawing is hidden,
   * with the project's own visually hidden rule, and never with display none,
   * which would take it out of the tree along with the pixels.
   *
   * The totals are a different case and stay visible, as the ring's own labels.
   */
  const stretchTable = (episode: Episode, source: string): HTMLElement => {
    const table = make("table");
    table.append(
      make(
        "caption",
        undefined,
        `Every stretch of the night ${source} recorded, in order. This is the drawing above in words.`,
      ),
    );
    const head = make("thead");
    const headRow = make("tr");
    for (const name of ["Started", "Ended", "Length", "What it called it"]) {
      const cell = make("th", undefined, name);
      cell.setAttribute("scope", "col");
      headRow.append(cell);
    }
    head.append(headRow);
    const body = make("tbody");
    for (const segment of episode.segments) {
      const row = make("tr");
      const start = make("th", undefined, formatClock(segment.startWallMs));
      start.setAttribute("scope", "row");
      row.append(start);
      row.append(make("td", undefined, formatClock(segment.endWallMs)));
      row.append(make("td", undefined, formatLength(segment.minutes)));
      row.append(make("td", undefined, segment.label));
      body.append(row);
    }
    table.append(head, body);
    return offScreen(table);
  };

  /** The ring in words, for the same reason: the ring is a drawing, and its
   * direct labels are text inside an aria-hidden drawing. */
  const sliceTable = (slices: NightSlice[], source: string): HTMLElement => {
    const table = make("table");
    table.append(
      make("caption", undefined, `The parts of the night ${source} recorded, added up.`),
    );
    const head = make("thead");
    const headRow = make("tr");
    for (const name of ["Part of the night", "Total"]) {
      const cell = make("th", undefined, name);
      cell.setAttribute("scope", "col");
      headRow.append(cell);
    }
    head.append(headRow);
    const body = make("tbody");
    for (const slice of slices) {
      const row = make("tr");
      const label = make("th", undefined, slice.label);
      label.setAttribute("scope", "row");
      row.append(label);
      row.append(make("td", undefined, formatLength(slice.minutes)));
      body.append(row);
    }
    table.append(head, body);
    return offScreen(table);
  };

  /** One source's night, drawn. Called again when the reader picks the other
   * source: the two are never on the page together. */
  const drawNight = (group: SourceNight, host: HTMLElement): void => {
    // Measured rather than assumed, because the ring is laid out in real pixels
    // so that its labels are the size they were asked to be on a phone as well
    // as on a laptop.
    const width = host.clientWidth || host.parentElement?.clientWidth || 320;
    shown = { group, host, width };
    host.replaceChildren();
    const episodes = episodesOf(group.segments);
    const night = longestEpisode(episodes);
    if (!night) return;
    const totals = summarise(night.segments);

    // The two times are the first and last moment this instrument had the
    // reader asleep, which is not the same as the ends of the stretch: a night
    // that opens with twenty minutes in bed and closes with eight minutes
    // awake would otherwise be reported as sleep that it never called sleep.
    const asleep = totals.asleepMinutes > 0;
    const started = formatClock(totals.firstAsleepWallMs ?? night.startWallMs);
    const ended = formatClock(totals.lastAsleepWallMs ?? night.endWallMs);
    const awake = totals.totals.find((total) => total.key === "awake");

    const lead = make("p");
    lead.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        asleep
          ? `${group.source} recorded ${formatLength(totals.asleepMinutes)} of sleep between ${started} and ${ended}, in ${night.segments.length} stretches`
          : `${group.source} recorded no sleep at all here, over ${night.segments.length} stretches between ${formatClock(night.startWallMs)} and ${formatClock(night.endWallMs)}`,
      ),
    );
    lead.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        awake
          ? `, ${formatLength(awake.minutes)} of which it marked awake. `
          : ". ",
      ),
    );
    lead.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        totals.inBedMinutes > 0
          ? `It has you in bed for ${formatLength(totals.inBedMinutes)}, which is a different thing and is not counted as sleep. `
          : "",
      ),
    );
    lead.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        totals.hasStages
          ? "The stage names are the instrument's own: it is guessing them from your wrist, and whether that guess is any good is week 3's question rather than this week's."
          : "This export names no sleep stages. Stages arrived with watchOS 9 and iOS 16, so an instrument older than that says in bed and asleep and nothing more. That is the whole of what it can say, not a fault in the file.",
      ),
    );
    host.append(lead);

    const rest = episodes.filter((episode) => episode !== night);
    if (rest.length > 0) {
      host.append(
        make(
          "p",
          undefined,
          `Also inside this window, and not part of that stretch: ${rest
            .map(
              (episode) =>
                `${formatLength((episode.endWallMs - episode.startWallMs) / 60_000)} from ${formatClock(episode.startWallMs)} to ${formatClock(episode.endWallMs)}`,
            )
            .join(", ")}. The window runs midday to midday, so it takes in the afternoon of the day you asked for.`,
        ),
      );
    }

    const carry = make("p");
    carry.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        asleep
          ? `To put this on the sheet above: press Asleep from here at ${started}, which is when this instrument first had you asleep. It last had you asleep at ${ended}, the next morning. `
          : `There is no time to carry up to the sheet from this source. It has you in bed and never has you asleep, which is all it was ever able to say. `,
      ),
    );
    carry.append(
      document.createTextNode(
        "The sheet has its own waking time at the top, typed when you started the day, and this page does not ask you to square the two. Putting a device's number next to your own and reporting the gap is week 3, and doing it now spends that week early.",
      ),
    );
    host.append(carry);

    const slices = sliceNight(night.segments);
    const lanes = laneFigure(night, group.source);
    host.append(lanes);
    addHourAxis(lanes, night);
    host.append(stretchTable(night, group.source));
    host.append(ringFigure(slices, group.source, width));
    host.append(sliceTable(slices, group.source));
  };

  // What is on the page now, so a window that changes width can have the ring
  // redrawn at the size it should have been. Held in a variable and nowhere
  // else: it goes with the tab, like everything here.
  let shown: { group: SourceNight; host: HTMLElement; width: number } | null = null;
  let resizeTimer = 0;

  window.addEventListener("resize", () => {
    window.clearTimeout(resizeTimer);
    resizeTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {
      if (!shown?.host.isConnected) return;
      const width = shown.host.clientWidth;
      // Redrawn only on a real change of width, since replacing the contents
      // of the host is itself something that could look like a resize.
      if (Math.abs(width - shown.width) < 24) return;
      drawNight(shown.group, shown.host);
    }, 150);
  });

  const render = (root: HTMLElement, segments: SleepSegment[], date: string): void => {
    const out = el<HTMLElement>(root, "[data-out]");
    if (!out) return;
    out.replaceChildren();
    out.hidden = false;

    const groups = groupBySource(segments);
    const heading = make("h4", undefined, `The night of ${formatCourseDate(date)}`);
    out.append(heading);

    const host = make("div");

    if (groups.length > 1) {
      // Two instruments wrote this night, which is ordinary: a phone and a
      // watch both keep sleep. They are not merged and they are not shown
      // together. One at a time, named, and the others said out loud so
      // nothing is quietly dropped.
      out.append(
        make(
          "p",
          undefined,
          `${groups.length} sources wrote records for this night: ${groups
            .map((group) => group.source)
            .join(", ")}. They are separate instruments and this page keeps them separate, so pick one. Reading two against each other is week 3's exercise and it is worth arriving at it without having done it already.`,
        ),
      );

      const fieldset = make("fieldset");
      fieldset.append(make("legend", undefined, "Whose record to read"));
      // The options in a row under their label, which is the same shape as
      // every other field in this component: a small uppercase name with its
      // control beneath it. The fieldset keeps the grouping for a screen
      // reader and gives up its box, since the only outline on the page is the
      // one around the whole component.
      const options = make("div", "night-sources");
      groups.forEach((group, index) => {
        const label = make("label");
        const radio = document.createElement("input");
        radio.type = "radio";
        radio.name = "night-source";
        radio.value = String(index);
        radio.checked = index === 0;
        radio.addEventListener("change", () => {
          if (radio.checked) drawNight(group, host);
        });
        label.append(radio, document.createTextNode(group.source));
        options.append(label);
      });
      fieldset.append(options);
      out.append(fieldset);
    } else {
      out.append(
        make("p", undefined, `One source wrote this night: ${groups[0].source}.`),
      );
    }

    out.append(host);
    drawNight(groups[0], host);
  };

  const readBlob = async (root: HTMLElement, blob: Blob, name: string): Promise<void> => {
    const date = dateValue(root);
    const nightWindow = nightWindowFor(date);
    if (!nightWindow) {
      say(root, "Set the date of the day you recorded first. This page opens one night, not a file.", "wrong");
      return;
    }
    if (reading) return;
    reading = true;
    readyToRead(root);
    clearOut(root);

    try {
      if (await looksLikeZip(blob)) {
        say(
          root,
          `${name} is a zip. Unzip it first and choose export.xml from inside: this page reads the XML rather than the archive, which is one instruction against a lot of code that would have to run over a file this size.`,
          "wrong",
        );
        return;
      }

      say(root, `Reading ${name}. Nothing is being sent anywhere.`);
      const result = await scanSleepWindow(blob, nightWindow, {
        onProgress: (read, total) =>
          progress(root, `Read ${megabytes(read)} of ${megabytes(total)}.`),
        // A slice at a time, with the page given a turn in between, so a
        // half gigabyte file does not freeze the tab while it is scanned.
        yieldBetweenChunks: () => new Promise((resume) => setTimeout(resume, 0)),
      });
      progress(root, null);

      if (result.sawClinicalDocument) {
        say(
          root,
          `${name} is the clinical document that ships in the same zip as the file this page wants. The one to choose is export.xml, which is the much larger of the two.`,
          "wrong",
        );
        return;
      }
      if (!result.sawHealthData && result.recordsSeen === 0) {
        say(root, `${name} does not look like an Apple Health export: there is no health data in it.`, "wrong");
        return;
      }
      if (result.sleepRecordsSeen === 0) {
        say(
          root,
          `${name} holds no sleep records at all, for any night. Nothing was ever written to Health by a watch, a phone or an app, so there is nothing here to read back and the sleep block on the sheet comes from memory like the rest of the day.`,
          "wrong",
        );
        return;
      }
      if (result.segments.length === 0) {
        say(
          root,
          `Nothing was recorded for the night of ${formatCourseDate(date)}. The file holds ${result.sleepRecordsSeen} sleep records from other nights, and this page will not open them. A night with nothing in it is a result: the sheet's sleep block comes from memory, which is what week 1 asks for anyway.`,
        );
        return;
      }

      render(root, result.segments, date);
      say(
        root,
        `Read ${megabytes(result.bytesRead)}. Of the ${result.sleepRecordsSeen} sleep records in the file, the ${result.segments.length} from that one night were kept and the rest went past without being held. None of it left the tab, and a reload loses this.`,
      );
    } catch (failure) {
      progress(root, null);
      say(
        root,
        `That file could not be read: ${failure instanceof Error ? failure.message : String(failure)}. Nothing was sent anywhere.`,
        "wrong",
      );
    } finally {
      reading = false;
      readyToRead(root);
    }
  };

  const setUp = (): void => {
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>("[data-sleep-export]");
    if (!root) return;
    chosen = null;
    reading = false;

    const file = el<HTMLInputElement>(root, "[data-night-file]");
    file?.addEventListener("change", () => {
      chosen = file.files?.[0] ?? null;
      clearOut(root);
      if (chosen) {
        say(
          root,
          `${chosen.name}, ${megabytes(chosen.size)}. Nothing has been read yet: press Read that night.`,
        );
      } else {
        say(root, "");
      }
      readyToRead(root);
    });

    el<HTMLInputElement>(root, "[data-night-date]")?.addEventListener("change", () => {
      // A new date is a new night, so the old one comes off the page rather
      // than sitting under a heading that no longer describes it.
      clearOut(root);
      readyToRead(root);
    });

    el<HTMLButtonElement>(root, "[data-action='read']")?.addEventListener("click", () => {
      if (chosen) void readBlob(root, chosen, chosen.name);
    });

    el<HTMLButtonElement>(root, "[data-action='sample']")?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const url = root.dataset.sample;
      if (!url || reading) return;
      // The night the sample was written around. Set here rather than asked
      // for, because a reader pressing this button wants to see the thing
      // work, not to guess which date the file has anything on.
      setDate(root, "2027-02-22");
      readyToRead(root);
      say(root, "Fetching the sample night from this site. It is generated and it is nobody's.");
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`the sample would not load (${response.status})`);
        await readBlob(root, await response.blob(), "the sample night");
      } catch (failure) {
        say(
          root,
          `The sample could not be fetched: ${failure instanceof Error ? failure.message : String(failure)}.`,
          "wrong",
        );
      }
    });

    readyToRead(root);
  };

  setUp();
  document.addEventListener("astro:page-load", setUp);
</script>
